RTL SoC integration evidence (#4), 2026-10-15
Top: muserisc_soc_top (in-house core + fabric + boot ROM +
CLINT + PLIC + 16550 UART + crypto bridge @0x2000_0000),
external async RAM modelled by tb/cpp/soc_rtl_tb.cpp.
All platform devices below are RTL, not the C++ soc_tb
model (which remains the Linux-boot harness).

1) On-core crypto driver + secp256k1 ECDSA verify
   ($ make -C sim socrtl; firmware sw/soc-tests/)
loaded 3688 bytes at 0x80000000 from build/soc-tests/crypto_soc_test.bin
SOC CRYPTO START
stage1 sha256 ok
stage2 field mul ok
stage3 2G ok
stage4a scalar inv ok
........
stage4b u1*G ok
........
stage4c u2*Q ok
SOC CRYPTO PASS
SOC RTL TB PASS (exit string at 11097819 cycles)
(wall 11m54s in Verilator; dots = 32 scalar bits each)
Stages: SHA-256("abc") vs known digest; field-mul mixed
vector mod secp p; Jacobian 2*G vs known coordinates;
ECDSA verify of the sw/ec_ops.py frozen vector (privkey
1, msg "museRISC", z hashed on the accelerator, scalar
inverse on the accelerator with MODSEL=2/MOD=n, point
muls with field ops on the accelerator). Ed25519 verify
is not yet ported (remains open on #4).

2) OpenSBI + S-mode payload on the RTL platform
   (soc_rtl_tb with fw_jump.bin at 0x80000000, DTB at
   0x83000000, s_hello.bin at 0x80200000; boot via the
   RTL boot ROM at 0x1000)
OpenSBI v1.9 banner over the RTL UART; platform devices
reported by OpenSBI: aclint-mswi, aclint-mtimer @
10000000Hz, uart8250; Domain0 Next Mode: S-mode; then:
HELLO FROM S-MODE
SOC RTL TB PASS (exit string at 5978097 cycles)
Full UART capture saved from the run (OpenSBI platform
and Boot HART lines) is reproduced in the session log;
the decisive lines are above.

Gates in the same session: make lint clean (0 warnings),
make test = ALL PHASE-0 KATs PASS, make -C sim core =
22/22 directed CORE TB PASS (includes the new
dmem_stall_i port tied off in the core harness).

Claim boundary: this proves the synthesizable SoC in
Verilator simulation. It is not a Linux boot on the RTL
SoC (Linux evidence remains the soc_tb C++ platform;
at this model's ~15k cycles/s a Linux boot would take
many hours), not timing closure (ASIC slice is #11),
and not FPGA hardware (#32).
